The '''Byrakans''' ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: Ⰱⰺⱃⰰⰽⰰⱜⰰ, ''Byrakaja''; [[Ottelian language|Ottelian]]: 𐐒𐐮𐑉𐐰𐐿𐐫𐑅, ''Byrakõs'') were the group of proto-historic peoples inhabiting [[Byrak]] from the {{abbr|14th century BCE|12th century BCE (Earth Year Equivalent)}} to the {{abbr|4th century|3rd century (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. This ethno-cultural concept, born from the [[Ottelians|Ottelian]] vision of [[Northern Meran]] (?), which was then little known to ancient geographers, is commonly used to designate all the various peoples who occupied the territories of present-day [[Qym]] and [[NEIGHBOR]]. 

From the {{abbr|5th century BCE|4th century BCE (Earth Year Equivalent)}} onwards, and especially following the [[Ottelia]]n conquest of [[Byrak]] in {{abbr|425 BCE|353 BCE (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, the Byrakans underwent a rapid process of acculturation due to extensive trade with and their subjugation to [[Ottelia]]. The markers of Byrakan civilization gradually faded, surviving only in limited fragments: the culture disappeared at the beginning of the 1st century AD, and the Byrakan language remained spoken but marginal, confined to religious texts, calendars, and votive dedications, in favor of the rise of [[Ottelian language|Ottelian]] in the region. Byrakan pantheons survived only partially, sometimes intermingled with the Ottelian deities that were becoming dominant in the public cults of the Byrakan cities under Ottelian rule and integrated into the subdivisions of Byrak. 

The Byrakans, strictly speaking, were politically composed of numerous independent nations and confederations speaking a range of Byrakan dialects. They believed they descended from a common ancestor, whose genealogy they knew, and were aware of some of the similarities that united them, much like what constituted Ottelian identity. To these ties of lineage, real or mythical, which sometimes created obligations of solidarity, asylum, and occasional military support, were regularly added alliances of convenience. As a result of these alliances and geopolitical balances, some Byrakan peoples became clients of other, more powerful peoples, forming tribal federations such as those of the [[Skuju]] and the [[Mishkas]]. All these peoples were divided into more or less defined political entities associated with a fixed territory, forming precursors to the administrative divisions of the Ottelian era. Most peoples had a capital, a political or religious center, surrounded by a neighboring territory containing secondary, rural communities. These structures sometimes persisted into Ottelian times which roughly correspond to modern-day Qymish counties. 

